WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.440
Okay,
we are going to go ahead and get started.

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:04.960
Thank you guys for coming.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.840
Again, another time slot
with a bit of competition.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:11.040
But you guys are the smart ones I can see.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.920
This is Simon Martinelli
who needs no introduction.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:20.960
If you've been involved in the Java
community, he is a legend there.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:22.600
And he's going to be talking about

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:26.480
some lessons
learned from spec driven development.

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:29.520
Let's give Simon a hand.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.400
Hey. Welcome everybody.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.400
Let me start by telling a story.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.240
So I'm in a sports club.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:45.480
I'm from Switzerland and I live in
a very small town and we do events.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.640
So we are mainly doing track and field,
and we have competitions for kids

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:56.360
already for 45 years and since 1997, I'm
doing software for them

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:01.120
because I realized that doing competition
and doing competition

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.280
ranking list and stuff like that
needs a lot of people.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:07.400
Back in the days,
like we have 12 to 15 people

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:11.160
and now it's just me using software,
doing that for them.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.240
But that's not the only thing that we do.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:17.840
We also have different events
and we always need volunteers.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:23.440
And it was in summer 2024
when we had public holiday in Switzerland.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.720
Someone of the club came to me and said,
hey Sam, we have an issue.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:31.320
We have volunteer management system, but
that's outdated and doesn't work anymore.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.720
So you're a software engineer
and I heard about this AI stuff.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:37.600
You can certainly do that
better. Let's go.

00:01:37.600 --> 00:01:39.000
And I said, okay, why not?

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.200
And I started to use windsurf.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.960
That was around fall 2024.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:46.000
And that was relatively fast.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:48.000
And I had a volunteer management system.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.840
But the problem was some one else
from Music Festival approached

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.520
me and said, hey, I heard that you're
building a volunteer management system.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:58.160
We need that as well.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:01:59.720
And then I had an issue.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:05.160
I had no clue what I was implementing,
and I had no no clue how I could change

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:09.680
the features so that it will fulfill
the requirements of this music festival.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.160
And this brought
me to spec-driven development

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.000
as I was already already introduced.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.600
I'm doing mostly Java development.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.880
I'm a consultant in Switzerland
for 17 years and I'm working for insurance

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:26.280
companies, wholesale, retail, government,

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.320
large enterprises mostly,
and I'm doing business applications.

00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:33.680
So I don't do tools, I don't do products, 
I do business applications.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:39.440
And during that time,
and I was kind of lost, I found a website.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.640
It was called AI native.

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:43.560
You may know it as Tessl.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.440
And I found an article from Simon Maple,

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:52.400
and he was writing about AI 90
development, and that kept my attention.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:55.400
And I read about spec-driven development.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.520
But at that time, spectrum
development was relatively new.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.040
So the term spectrum
development is very old, right?

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.480
That's from the 2000 or maybe late 90s.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.920
But the spec-driven development with
AI was relatively new.

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:12.080
So I started to think about
what could specs be.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.680
And because I'm doing business
applications, I had a different take,

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:21.600
probably on spec-driven development,
and I tried to find a way to do that.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:25.640
And so in the meanwhile,
if we look at the spec-driven development

00:03:25.840 --> 00:03:30.000
ecosystem,
there are flavors of spec-driven development.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.000
And there is one process centric.

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:33.040
That's my process.

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.680
I created this a unified process.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:37.640
I will show you that in a minute.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:39.160
But on the other hand, we have tools.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:42.960
We have Amazon Kiro, we have GitHub
spec kit, we have BMT method.

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:44.560
These are all great tools.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:48.320
And also Tesla created
spec-driven tools back then.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:53.240
Sorry, 2025,
but they are all, in my opinion,

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:56.280
at least to developer centric.

00:03:56.760 --> 00:04:01.040
And I try to cover the whole software
development

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.520
lifecycle, especially for enterprises
where you not are solar planner.

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:08.560
You're someone in a larger team

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:12.080
with different roles
in a large organization,

00:04:12.280 --> 00:04:18.160
and there's a website called
AI Unified Process.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:19.520
Let me switch that.

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.040
And there you will see kind of a diagram
with some colors.

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.560
So the colors mean that
you usually if you're on a green field

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:30.840
I will talk about brownfield as well
because I'm rarely on greenfield projects.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:34.760
You start with a vision
and then you start gathering requirements.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.560
However you would do that.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:38.720
By the way
there is the International Requirements

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:43.440
Engineering Board and they just created
something called a microgrid angel.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.800
It's called
AI for requirements engineering.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:47.920
And they have prompting guide
skills and stuff.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:49.040
They are working on that.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:53.200
How requirements
engineer can be speed up with AI.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:55.200
And then we have testing and stuff
like that.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:59.040
And the green thing
is probably what's interesting here.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.960
I was thinking about what could be good
specs that A understands,

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:08.600
but also all stakeholders in the project
can understand.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.080
And I ended up with system
use cases by the way, who knows.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:15.120
System use cases? Who knows that?

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:16.720
Not a lot of people as I saw.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:19.240
So system use cases were created by

00:05:20.240 --> 00:05:23.240
back in 1987.

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:27.520
And then later on
we had UML and ROP and stuff like that.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:32.040
That was that movement, 
late 80s, early 90s.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:37.040
But I was using this muse cases
for when I was working for Swiss Railways

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:42.160
early 2000, and this quite worked
well as a communication

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:45.480
specification between stakeholders
and developers back then.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:46.520
And I thought, okay,

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:50.200
if that worked back then,
why shouldn't it work together with AI?

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.080
And then we need something else.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.600
I call it the entity model,
but it's more like a domain model.

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:59.280
So it depends if you're doing domain
driven design or how you work.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:03.560
These are the more or less
usually the data at the end.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:07.440
But we need to model
that and these things together

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:10.680
with some software architecture
I will talk about that later.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:15.720
Can be used to directly generate code,
because the difference from my approach

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:19.680
to the tool approach is the tool
approach is always the same.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:21.960
So usually if you look at Amazon Kiro

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.520
for example,
you have product requirements document.

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:29.440
From that you generate some plan
and out of the plan there will be tasks.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:32.200
And finally AI will implement the tasks.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:34.320
I skipped the plant task phase.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:39.080
I just use communication entity
model and generate code directly.

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:44.040
So now this music is are not enough
because usually there's a UI for example.

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:47.080
Or if you build an API,
you have an API spec.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.440
So you need additional information
to that.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:53.600
So the system use case
just defines the behavior.

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:58.040
But how it looks can be, for example,
a Figma design that you can integrate

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:02.040
with MCP server and directly interact
with Figma out of that.

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:05.800
And then usually code is generated
and test is generated.

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:08.200
The question is in which order.

00:07:08.200 --> 00:07:11.680
So I'm mostly doing 
full stack development.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:13.200
That means I have a UI.

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:15.320
And if you have a UI, test driven

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:18.560
development is difficult
because you first need to figure out how

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:22.080
the UI will look like before
you can start to create the tests.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:26.400
If you do APIs, for example, I would
always go for test driven development.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.960
So start with the tests and then use the
tests also to derive the code generation.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:36.080
And finally, as we can see always
there is a review phase.

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:39.200
So most often things are reviewed.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:41.680
The question is
how much review do you need.

00:07:41.680 --> 00:07:44.160
And that's all about risk management.

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:45.880
So I'm currently working

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:48.920
on the modernization of an ERP system
for the largest wholesale

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:50.200
company in Switzerland.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:54.720
And if you look at the near system,
you have different modules

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:58.000
in the system
that don't have all the same.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:00.640
How should I say criticality.

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:04.440
So that means if you have the product
management or the inventory part,

00:08:04.480 --> 00:08:05.640
if that doesn't work,

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:09.440
that's probably not even a problem,
because people that are working with

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:13.520
that inventory management system,
they can just go and grab a coffee.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:16.760
If the auto management system
doesn't work, that's

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:22.760
more of a problem because then the company
probably will lose money, right?

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.600
So you should do risk management

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:28.840
and then decide how much review your code
probably needs.

00:08:28.840 --> 00:08:30.560
But that's not different from AI

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:34.360
or manual driven development
that that's just how it works.

00:08:34.360 --> 00:08:37.840
Now let's have a look in the detail.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:40.720
So if we have a greenfield
project for example.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:45.520
We would start with with the specs.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:47.440
So the specs means
we have some requirements.

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:50.680
Engineers,
product owners, business analyst.

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:55.840
Depending on how
the people are called in your organization

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:58.280
and they create the entity model
and use cases.

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:02.000
So they can also derive that directly
from requirements, for example.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:06.160
And then we will have a phase
where we can review that.

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:10.280
So there will be some thing
like definition of done.

00:09:10.280 --> 00:09:14.480
And the software engineer together with AI
and the requirements engineer

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:18.880
will decide when the use case is done
or the specification is done.

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:22.400
And then we use the agent
and the agent comes with.

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:25.720
But a lot of people
were already talking about.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:30.440
So we need skills MCP guidelines,
guardrails, whatever.

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:32.840
We will see that in a minute in a demo.

00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:38.880
But because we don't do the plan and task
phase, we need that in the middle.

00:09:38.920 --> 00:09:43.400
That is probably the most important thing
of the whole process.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:47.280
So that means the skill
must match the outcome.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:52.120
That means we need skills that knows how
the code and test should be generated.

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:54.280
And that's not always the same.

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:57.840
So I'm working currently for six customers
with that process.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:01.240
And all the skills are different
because I have customers

00:10:01.240 --> 00:10:04.640
that use React and Spring Boot
or just use and Spring Boot

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:09.200
that's a framework
or are using angular and quarks.

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:12.960
So I have a variety
of different frameworks

00:10:12.960 --> 00:10:17.520
and also some in-house frameworks

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:22.280
maybe that need to be working in
in that context.

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:26.760
Now that's a greenfield project,
but I really rarely do that.

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:29.480
What I do
mostly is software modernization.

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:34.680
So I'm doing modernization for enterprise
applications for about eight years now.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.240
And there I just changed the process.

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:43.360
That means I extract

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:46.600
use case and entity model
from code and tests and documentation.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:50.200
Maybe I have confidence here or whatever,
because usually the documentation

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:54.480
is spread around
multiple artifacts that we have,

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:56.760
and then we have the entity model
and the use cases.

00:10:56.760 --> 00:11:00.280
They will be revised
or reviewed by the business people.

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:05.000
And then we generate the new code,
because there are a lot of IDs

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:08.760
that we can directly transform,
maybe from COBOL to travel.

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:12.280
So that's also something
that anthropic is, is telling us.

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:13.760
But that never worked.

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:19.680
So we did that like 30 years ago
COBOL to C, or COBOL to C++, or something.

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:24.080
But that's not helpful
because that's just lift and shift.

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:26.120
And modernization is not lift and shift.

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:29.520
Modernization is rethinking
how people are working

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:33.320
with the software, integrating features
that maybe are not there.

00:11:33.320 --> 00:11:37.600
And because of this reverse engineering,
we also have

00:11:38.400 --> 00:11:42.920
a positive feedback from the end users
because we are not transforming

00:11:42.920 --> 00:11:48.800
from one technology to another, because
regarding our use case and entity model.

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:52.480
And we can integrate new features
because usually you don't do that.

00:11:52.480 --> 00:11:56.920
So the guideline when we started
with the modernization project

00:11:56.960 --> 00:12:00.800
two years ago was
we want to have the exact same system

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:02.080
just in another technology,

00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:05.960
and you don't add new features because
we don't want to introduce new bugs.

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:11.200
And now we can just do that
because we just change the specification.

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:14.840
Now the question is maybe why use cases?

00:12:14.880 --> 00:12:17.760
Because user
usually people are using stories

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:20.920
or any other product requirements
document.

00:12:20.960 --> 00:12:23.920
The point is use
cases are very well defined

00:12:23.920 --> 00:12:29.320
and even AI knows how to write use cases
because it's around for a very long time.

00:12:29.320 --> 00:12:32.560
That means we have use case
that has usually that form, right.

00:12:32.600 --> 00:12:37.080
We have precondition post conditions
and we have scenarios.

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:41.440
Usually you have a main success scenario
and then you have alternative flows.

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:45.200
And if we compare that with user stories
we can see that user

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:49.400
stories are just a collection or use cases
a collection of user story usually.

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:51.760
So one user story is usually

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:56.000
a flow in a use case.

00:12:56.000 --> 00:12:59.680
And in my opinion
or in my experience, use

00:12:59.680 --> 00:13:02.960
cases are better than user stories
because they are simply bigger.

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:05.280
And so we have that.

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:07.440
And I created a small project.

00:13:07.440 --> 00:13:09.560
So do we have Java developers here?

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:11.000
By the way?

00:13:11.000 --> 00:13:16.800
You certainly know the application
that I was already displaying before.

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:18.000
We have the Spring PetClinic.

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:21.520
So that's kind of a demo project
from the spring framework.

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:23.040
Just some history.

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:27.360
Spring was using Pet Clinic
because before we had Travel

00:13:27.360 --> 00:13:31.520
Enterprise Edition and they had Pet Shop
and they wanted to

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:36.160
stay in this pet industry,
so to say, decorated that.

00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:40.600
So what we can do here,
we can see the doctors, we can find

00:13:40.640 --> 00:13:44.960
owners and owners have pets, for example,
Eduardo Rodriguez,

00:13:45.160 --> 00:13:50.040
Stool and Rosie,
and for example, Julie's here and

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:54.240
has a broken leg.

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:56.400
And we can add it.

00:13:56.400 --> 00:13:58.000
So that's more or less that.

00:13:58.000 --> 00:13:59.440
That's very simple,

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:02.680
a very, very simple application,
but it's more or less what I'm doing.

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:06.360
So I don't do that simple applications,
but I'm doing business applications.

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:08.640
So we have UIs, data, stuff like that.

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:11.640
And what I did I reverse engineered

00:14:12.880 --> 00:14:17.680
the pet clinic and that's a use case
UML diagram by the way.

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:20.680
And this is quite helpful
because on the diagram we have actors.

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:23.160
So you have two the visitor
and the clinic user.

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:26.320
And these usually
are also roles in the system.

00:14:26.360 --> 00:14:32.240
And then we have like use cases
they are grouped into parts or in modules

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:32.720
whatever.

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:37.080
So we have welcome page with the doctors
then the owner management

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:38.160
the pet management.

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:43.400
And finally they visit
or the management that I just did.

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:47.360
And when I do reverse engineering
I just go on that level.

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:48.640
We also have an entity model.

00:14:48.640 --> 00:14:51.240
So that's usually derived
from the database model.

00:14:51.240 --> 00:14:53.680
It depends if you have a SQL database
or other database,

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:57.440
but usually you have information
to cover that.

00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:01.520
And here we have a diagram
that just contains the types

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:04.880
that we can see
how things are related in the system.

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:07.880
And then we have definition of
of the entities.

