00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:45.680
In my opinion, the architecture
has an impact on how well things work.

00:17:45.680 --> 00:17:48.840
So first of all, in the past

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:51.880
15 years or so,
we were doing microservices

00:17:52.040 --> 00:17:57.400
and most of my customers
did microservices in a very naive way.

00:17:57.600 --> 00:18:01.760
So they have way too many microservices
because they were focusing

00:18:01.760 --> 00:18:04.760
on the micro in microservices.

00:18:04.880 --> 00:18:06.280
And that's probably a problem.

00:18:06.280 --> 00:18:08.880
So they have this distributed
big bowl of mud.

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:13.440
So I'm working for an insurance company
and they have around 500 microservices.

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:18.240
And that's kind of an impact
because they also have kind of

00:18:18.280 --> 00:18:20.720
500 micro front ends.

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:23.280
And they are kind of related. Right.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:28.600
So we have an end to end relationship
between front end and pack end.

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:31.240
And that's the worst case scenario.

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:33.760
Because if you have
if you want to work with

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:37.720
AI on a certain part of your system,
you have to have the context.

00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:42.160
So you need the code that the
AI should work on in a single place,

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:45.960
at least on your machine
or wherever you work with that with AI.

00:18:46.280 --> 00:18:49.800
And if you have five microservices,
a lot of components somewhere,

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:53.920
and you have to mix and match that,
that becomes very difficult.

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:57.440
Now there's a movement away
from microservices

00:18:57.440 --> 00:19:01.560
to monolithic or modular
monolithic application.

00:19:01.560 --> 00:19:07.960
And I would say stop here, don't do that
because that's not the right way,

00:19:08.200 --> 00:19:12.920
because maybe you have like
a huge monolith that we have

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:18.160
currently pieces as thousands of database
tables and a lot of modules.

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:19.720
And that's a problem, right?

00:19:19.720 --> 00:19:21.280
Because the context is too big.

00:19:21.280 --> 00:19:23.880
So how can we work on that?

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:26.960
And there's an architecture style
that not so well known, but

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:33.440
it's approximately created
when microservices were created.

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:34.920
It's called self-contained system.

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:38.920
And the self-contained system architecture
just says we create verticals.

00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:42.960
So we split our application
into verticals, which has UIs, business

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:46.400
logic and database in one repo usually,

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:49.440
or in one project at least, where is one.

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:52.920
And if you have that,
if you can split that and we do that

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:58.200
with the modernization,
then the AI can exactly work on that.

00:19:58.200 --> 00:20:03.600
And you can also add skills in depending
on what technology you're using.

00:20:03.600 --> 00:20:07.600
So for example, in the inventory system
here we are using voting as I do

00:20:07.600 --> 00:20:08.200
in my demo.

00:20:08.200 --> 00:20:11.640
That's a web framework in Java.

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:15.280
But for the order management system
for example we have react in the frontend.

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:18.960
So we can have different technology
in different self-contained systems.

00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:23.080
There's another thing
that if you can stay on one stack.

00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:27.480
So for example, if you stay with the Java
stack or stay with the JavaScript

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:32.360
TypeScript stack or ecosystem,
then probably creating order is simpler

00:20:32.360 --> 00:20:37.080
because you need to create skills
and all the stuff just for one technology.

00:20:37.120 --> 00:20:39.760
Otherwise,
if you do what my customers usually doing,

00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:44.080
they have a single page
framework like React or Angular,

00:20:44.080 --> 00:20:46.920
and then usually spring boot
or quarks in the backend,

00:20:46.920 --> 00:20:51.920
they have to create this twice
and also maintain that twice, right?

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:54.240
And once.

00:20:54.240 --> 00:20:59.360
Another thing that's also quite
interesting is the impact on the teams.

00:20:59.360 --> 00:21:01.640
And that's the big change.

00:21:01.640 --> 00:21:07.280
So for those a company that's relatively
simple because that's not a huge team.

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:10.440
And they already work in maintenance mode.

00:21:10.440 --> 00:21:12.920
So they don't really do scrum anymore
right.

00:21:12.920 --> 00:21:14.400
They don't have fixed sprints.

00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:17.960
They work on depending on what features
they have to implement.

00:21:17.960 --> 00:21:21.120
But because we

00:21:21.120 --> 00:21:24.920
use like the specs as an input,
as we also kind of

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:28.080
did with the stories,
by the way, in a, in a scrum

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:31.360
development way.

00:21:32.040 --> 00:21:36.440
But we are very fast
so we cannot wait to weeks.

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:37.920
So that's way too long.

00:21:37.920 --> 00:21:40.520
So we need
maybe two weeks to do the specification,

00:21:40.520 --> 00:21:43.840
but we don't need to weeks
to create the software.

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:46.040
And the other is reduce the team size.

00:21:46.040 --> 00:21:49.920
We are per self-contained system,
1 to 2 developers,

00:21:50.240 --> 00:21:53.600
preferable two
because of knowledge exchange.

